node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/02-guides/optimizing-prefetching.md:
  Line 201: Awaiting [`unstable_navigation()`](/docs/app/api-reference/functions/navigation) above the work leaves it out of every prefetch, so it is produced on the navigation. The nearest [`<Suspense>`](https://react.dev/reference/react/Suspense) fallback is what the user sees while it streams in. Take the dashboard again, where a ranking keyed on the session is too expensive to produce for every link into the route:
  Line 205: import { unstable_navigation } from 'next/cache'
  Line 209:   await unstable_navigation()
  Line 220: The session is read above `unstable_navigation()`, so it resolves in the shell like any other session data. Only the ranking is left out, and it keeps its cache lifetime.
  Line 222: Without it, keeping a subtree out of a prefetch means leaving it uncached, which gives up that lifetime on the navigation too. Awaiting `unstable_navigation()` inside a cached scope is not allowed, so put the caching directive on a function called below it, as `rank` does here.
  Line 226: Unlike [`connection()`](/docs/app/api-reference/functions/connection), `unstable_navigation()` does not make the subtree request-dependent. This lets reusable content keep its cache lifetime while waiting for navigation. See [How `unstable_navigation()` differs from `connection()`](/docs/app/api-reference/functions/navigation#how-unstable_navigation-differs-from-connection) for the full comparison.
  Line 294: - [`unstable_prefetch()`](/docs/app/api-reference/functions/prefetch) and [`unstable_navigation()`](/docs/app/api-reference/functions/navigation) for the full behavior of each prefetch stage.

node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/03-api-reference/04-functions/navigation.md:
  Line 2: title: unstable_navigation
  Line 3: description: API Reference for the unstable_navigation function, which keeps a subtree out of a route's prefetches so it renders when the user navigates.
  Line 15: Calling `await unstable_navigation()` defers the code below it to the navigation, instead of rendering it when a link to the route is prefetched. The fallback of the nearest [`<Suspense>`](https://react.dev/reference/react/Suspense) boundary takes its place in the prefetch, and the deferred content renders when the user navigates.
  Line 17: It is meant to defer content that a prefetch would otherwise render, even content that would sit in the App Shell, typically because it depends on session data or URL data and is expensive to generate. During a build-time prerender nothing is deferred, so content below `unstable_navigation()` is prerendered as usual.
  Line 40: Awaiting `unstable_navigation()` keeps content out of the App Shell and out of per-link prefetches, so it renders only on the navigation.
  Line 42: Without Partial Prefetching, `unstable_navigation()` still defers the content for a default `<Link>`, but [`<Link prefetch={true}>`](/docs/app/api-reference/components/link#prefetch) fetches the whole route, deferred content included, the same as a navigation.
  Line 46: Everything above `unstable_navigation()` renders in the prefetch, and everything below renders on the navigation. Deferral is scoped to the component that awaits it and the tree below: sibling branches render in the prefetch as usual. Place it above the code to defer, and wrap the component in [`<Suspense>`](https://react.dev/reference/react/Suspense):
